<h1>Gerardo Tristan, Jr.</h1>
<p>Judge up for retention, on the Chicago ballot November 3, 2026. Incumbent.</p>
<p>Circuit Court of Cook County</p>
<h2>Ratings</h2>
<ul>
<li>Chicago Bar Association: <a href="https://www.chicagobar.org/votejudges#:~:text=Gerardo%20Tristan%2C%20Jr." rel="nofollow noopener">Qualified</a></li>
<li>Illinois State Bar Association: <a href="https://www.isba.org/judicial-elections/counties/cook#:~:text=Gerardo%20Tristan%2C%20Jr." rel="nofollow noopener">Yes</a></li>
<li>Injustice Watch: <a href="https://2026retention.injusticewatch.org/candidate/gerardo-tristan-jr-35901" rel="nofollow noopener">Former prosecutor</a></li>
<li>Injustice Watch: <a href="https://2026retention.injusticewatch.org/candidate/gerardo-tristan-jr-35901" rel="nofollow noopener">Notable controversy</a></li>
</ul>
<h2>Record</h2>
<p>Appointed to the Circuit Court in January 2019 and elected in 2020; hears civil jury cases, including evictions, personal injury, and small claims, in the First Municipal District. Injustice Watch reports that in 2023 his parents&#39; caregiver sued him and a brother, alleging she was not paid $240 for her last three days of work and that he stopped her from leaving the home; the suit settled in May 2024 for an undisclosed amount. His brother told a 911 operator that day that he seemed intoxicated, and the police report says officers saw no signs of intoxication; he told Injustice Watch he was angry but denied stopping her from leaving or being intoxicated. A judge stays on the bench with a yes from 60 percent of voters.</p>
<h2>Background</h2>
<p>Cook County assistant state&#39;s attorney prosecuting felony and juvenile delinquency cases (2003-2019); attorney at his brother&#39;s law firm, incorporating businesses and defending employers in labor lawsuits (2002-2003). Law degree from DePaul University (2002).</p>
